# Database — Access & Safety Defaults

Created: 2026-02-02 05:37 UTC  
Last reviewed: 2026-02-02 05:37 UTC

This entry describes a safe, repeatable approach to accessing databases during investigations.

## Default Constraints
- Use read-only credentials by default.
- Add a `LIMIT` (or enforce one).
- Use time windows in predicates (e.g., “last 7 days”).
- Set statement timeouts (server-side if available; always client-side).

## Postgres (Local)
```sh
psql "postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/app"
```

## Postgres (Cloud) — Typical Pattern
Common patterns (varies by org):
- Connect via bastion / VPN
- Or tunnel via SSM

Example (tunnel):
```sh
# placeholder: establish tunnel first, then:
psql "postgres://readonly@127.0.0.1:5432/app"
```

## Query Safety
- Prefer selecting specific columns over `SELECT *`.
- Avoid unbounded joins on large tables.
- Treat “write-like” SQL as blocked unless explicitly approved (`INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE/DDL`).

## Evidence Capture (What to Record)
- env (`local/staging/prod`), db name, role (readonly/write)
- query template + params (redacted)
- row count returned + whether truncated
- duration, timeout, errors

